{
  "method_card": {
    "steps": [
      "Identify the type: find %, increase/decrease, reverse, or express as %",
      "For increase/decrease: find the multiplier (1 ± percentage/100)",
      "For reverse: divide the new amount by the multiplier",
      "For express as %: divide part by whole, multiply by 100"
    ],
    "title": "How to Work with Percentages",
    "content": "<p><strong>Finding a percentage of an amount:</strong> Convert the percentage to a decimal (÷ 100) and multiply. For example, 15% of £80 = 0.15 × 80 = £12.</p><p><strong>Percentage increase/decrease:</strong> Use a <strong>multiplier</strong>. For an increase, add the percentage to 100%: e.g. 20% increase -> multiplier = 1.20. For a decrease, subtract: 20% decrease -> 0.80. Multiply the original by the multiplier.</p><p><strong>Express one number as a percentage of another:</strong> Divide the part by the whole, then multiply by 100: \\(\\frac{\\text{part}}{\\text{whole}} \\times 100\\).</p><p><strong>Reverse percentages:</strong> You're given the amount <em>after</em> the increase or decrease and need to find the original. Identify the multiplier, then divide: \\(\\text{Original} = \\frac{\\text{new amount}}{\\text{multiplier}}\\). For example, after a 20% increase the price is £90: original = 90 ÷ 1.2 = £75.</p><p><strong>Percentage change:</strong> \\(\\frac{\\text{change}}{\\text{original}} \\times 100\\).</p>",
    "example": "<p><strong>A coat costs £60 after a 25% discount. What was the original price?</strong></p><p><strong>Step 1 — Identify type:</strong> Reverse percentage (given the reduced price, find original).</p><p><strong>Step 2 — Find the multiplier:</strong> 25% decrease -> 100% − 25% = 75% -> 0.75</p><p><strong>Step 3 — Divide:</strong> \\(\\text{Original} = \\frac{60}{0.75} = 80\\)</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> The original price was <strong>£80</strong></p>"
  },
